    "summary": "Fire‑lit Jack Straw, massive He's Gone → Truckin' → The Other One jam",
    "blurb": "First set flows smoothly with Row Jimmy and a tight Weather Report Suite before the second set launches into a marathon He's Gone → Truckin' → The Other One sequence and a soaring Eyes Of The World. Jerry’s guitar is on fire, Phil’s bass thunderous, and the overall energy stays high throughout.",
    "review": "On November 23, 1973 at the County Coliseum in El Paso, the Dead delivered one of their most exhilarating late‑'73 outings. A relaxed first set gave us classics like Row Jimmy, a seamless Weather Report Suite Prelude → Part 1 → Let It Grow, and a bright China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider pairing, while the second half exploded into an extended He's Gone → Truckin' → The Other One jam that rolls straight into Me And Bobby McGee before launching into an inspired Eyes Of The World. Jerry Garcia was “on fire” all night—his tone crisp on Row Jimmy and shredding through The Other One, with a powerful lead on Jack Straw—while Phil Lesh’s thunderous bass underpinned the improvisations, moving the audience physically. Keyboardist Keith Godchaux is audible enough to lay down his signature two‑note riff beneath the long jams, though some listeners note occasional mix ambiguity. Bill Kreutzmann drives the high‑energy sections with a fast, bebop‑inflected rhythm, especially during The Other One. All three soundboard recordings capture this magic, but the Clevenger SBD stands out for its pristine clarity and lack of dropouts, making it the definitive version to hear.",
